Gavin77 wrote:
> Hi,

Hello!

> I have spent about 3 months playing nethack on and off.
> Seems like a really cool game.

It is.

> A good website I have found is http://www.steelypips.org/nethack
> I have been using that for information to help. As I really need it. I
> have never gotten past level 10 in the dungeon. I usually play a Barb
> or Wiz.

Barbarians and Valkyries are the two easiest classes to play.  Most
people seem to have their first ascension with one of those two
classes.  Although Wizards often start with some nice equipment and a
cloak of magic resistance (in practice very useful), they don't have
much hitting power or hit points at the start.

> There are some things that really annoy me in this game. Its all very
> well making it challenging, but there comes a point where it makes the
> game a real pain to play and not fun. I would appreicate some comments
> on the below. Thanks.

Well let's have a look.

> Pet does not always follow you into places
> It is often hard to get your Pet next to the staircase in a dungeon
> Pets often do nothing when they are next to you and you have multiple
> monsters attacking you. They only seem to help sometimes ??

There are items that can help you with that.  And note that pets are
good for much more that just fighting monsters....

> There are too many secrets walls/doors in dungeons. I find it virtually
> impossible to reveal everything on a level without getting hungry. So I
> often miss out on shops etc on levels. You can find and put a ring of
> searching on, but it doesnt always reveal things straight away. And you
> get more hungry if you wear magic rings.

Time will give you a better sense of where to search for secret doors.
Remember you can eat many dead monsters, and some of them are even
advantageous to munch on, so long as you eat the monster very soon.
(There are some monster corpses that aren't so healthy, it's true,
although Barbarians can eat a bit more, by default, than other classes.
 This is one of those things your pet can help you with by the way....)

One of the possible rings you can find is a ring of slow digestion,
which might come in handy.  Overall, you'll find that starving will
become less of a problem as you get practice in making every move
count.

> The biggest problem I have with hunger is that it is increased when you
> are using a magic ring or amulet. Could somebody tell me if wielding a
> magic weapon or if wearing magic armor increases hunger also ??

They don't.  And some rings make you hungrier faster than others.  (And
one I've already mentioned greatly *decreases* the rate at which you
grow hungry.)

> It is way too easy to get your good items corroded. I have discovered
> that bashing acid and ocre jelly with my hands is better. But Gush
> Water traps are common, and there is no way of knowing the trap is
> there ?? I heard you can dip corroded weapons in potions of oil to fix
> them, but I am sure you cannot dip armor ?

I think any rusty item can be repaired by dipping in oil, but in the
long run you'll be better served by having your stuff rustproofed or
using items that cannot rust.  steelypips can help out with that.
(hint: check the entry on scrolls....)  I don't think corrosion is the
same as rusting by the way, although its effects on your game are
similar.  Blessed equipment tends to get damaged in this way much less
often than uncursed.

> I heard you can grease your
> items to prevent this, but is it 100% proof against corroding ?

It is, I believe, until the grease wears off.

> Its not
> a good solution anyway, because the grease soon wears off? And any
> weapon you grease gets constantly dropped - not good while in a battle.

Yeah, although grease can help out out in battle with some particular
highly dangerous monsters you'll be meeting later....

